n\nAnother thought: The task name \"SE-CR-02\" might be from some known problem set (maybe from a testing platform). \"CR\" could stand for \"Coverage Reduction\" or \"Critical Reduction\". The \"attempts\" might be the number of times we tested removal. \"one_minimal\" could be true if the minimal set is exactly one item? No, in case 0, minimal set has two items, so one_minimal false. In case 1, required_failure_items = [\"t1\",\"t4\"], minimal set would be [\"t1\",\"t4\"], so one_minimal false. In case 2, also two items. So all have one_minimal false. But maybe the algorithm doesn't always result in exactly the required items; there could be more items that are needed due to interdependencies? But here there are no interdependencies; any item not in required can be removed. So reduced_items would be exactly required_failure_items. Then one_minimal is false because there are multiple items (2). So that fits.\n\nBut attempts: possibly the number of times we try to remove an item. Starting with 10 items, we try to remove each in order. For case 0, we try t0 (fail), t1 (success, remove), then continue with the remaining list (t2..t9). After removing t1, we now have 9 items. Next we try t2? But the algorithm might start over from the beginning after each removal? Or continue sequentially. If we continue sequentially, after t1 removal, the next item is t2 (still original index 2). We try t2: not required, remove. Then t3, etc. So we remove all non-required items in the order they appear. That would be 8 successful removals (t1..t8) and 2 failures (t0 and t9). Total attempts = 10. But attempts might be the number of removal attempts (including failures) = 10. That seems plausible.\n\nAlternatively, attempts could be the number of iterations of a while loop that scans the list until no more removable items. In that case, we have one pass that removes all non-required, so number of iterations = 1? But attempts is integer, likely larger.\n\nLet's test case 1: required_failu