n\nWhat about if the required_failure_items are not necessarily a subset? They are, as per payload.\n\nThus, a straightforward implementation: \ndef solve(case):\n    items = case[\"items\"]\n    required = set(case[\"required_failure_items\"])\n    attempts = 0\n    # implement greedy removal: iterate over list, attempt to remove if not required\n    result = items[:]  # copy\n    i = 0\n    while i < len(result):\n        attempts += 1\n        item = result[i]\n        if item not in required:\n            # try removing: check if condition still holds (i.e., all required still present)\n            # Since we are only removing non-required, condition holds -> remove\n            result.pop(i)\n            # do not increment i because next item shifts to current index\n        else:\n            i += 1\n    # After loop, result should contain exactly the required items in original order.\n    one_minimal = len(result) == 1\n    return {\n        \"attempts\": attempts,\n        \"one_minimal\": one_minimal,\n        \"reduced_items\": result\n    }\n\nThis algorithm will have attempts = number of items considered, which is exactly the initial length of the list (10) because each item is checked exactly once: when we encounter a non-required, we remove it and stay at same index (which now has the next item). So all items are checked exactly once. So attempts = len(items) = 10 for all cases. That yields attempts=10 for all three. But is that always true? If there were no required items, then all would be removed and loop would go through all. So attempts always equals initial length? Actually, consider a case where items list has duplicates? Not here. Or if required items appear multiple times? Not. So attempts = len(items) always. But the problem might have more complex scenarios where attempts can vary. However, given the public fixtures are identical in length, we can't tell.\n\nMaybe the algorithm is different: It might try to remove items in a random order? But no randomness allowed.\n\nAnother possibility: The reduction uses a specific method like \"try to remove each item, but only succeed if the set still contains all required items and also some other condition\". But no other condition given.\n\nI recall a known task from some coding challenge: \"SE-CR-02\" might be from a test suite about \"critical items reduction\". Perhaps the reduction is based on a \"failure\" condition defined by a function that is not exposed in the public fixture. But the public fixture only provides items and required_failure_items. Could it be that the actual test will provide a function? No, the payload only has those keys.\n\nWait, the instructions say: \"Do not expect case_id, seed, or any outer fixture-row keys; use only fields present in the payload object.\" So the payload contains only \"items\", \"required_failure_items\", and \"_case_nonce\". So we must use those.\n\nGiven the limited info, I think the above algorithm is the most plausible. However, we need to be careful: The problem might involve \"failure\" being defined as the presence of at least one of the required_failure_items? Or maybe all? The term \"required_failure_items\" suggests they are all required to cause failure. So condition: all required_failure_items are in the set.\n\nLet's check with case 0: required = {\"t0\",\"t9\"}. If we remove t0, condition fails because t0 missing. So we cannot remove t0 or t9. So final set is exactly {\"t0\",\"t9\"}.\n\nWhat about if there were items that are not required but if removed, the set still contains all required? That is always true. So all non-required are removable. So the greedy algorithm will remove all non-required, leaving only required items. So reduced_items = required_failure_items in original order.\n\nNow, one_minimal: false because length 2.\n\nNow, attempts: The algorithm above counts each check as an attempt. Since we check each item exactly once, attempts = initial length = 10. That is consistent.\n\nBut let's verify with the loop: Starting len=10, i=0. Check t0 (required): i=1; attempts=1. t1 (not required): remove, len=9, i stays 1; attempts=2. Now list: t0,t2,t3,...t9. i=1 now points to t2 (since t1 removed). t2 not required: remove, len=8, i=1; attempts=3. t3: remove, len=7; attempts=4. t4: remove, len=6; attempts=5. t5: remove, len=5; attempts=6. t6: remove, len=4; attempts=7. t7: remove, len=3; attempts=8. t8: remove, len=2; attempts=9. Now list: t0, t9. i=1 (points to t9). t9 required: i=2; attempts=10. Loop ends because i=2 >= len=2. So attempts=10.\n\nSo yes.\n\nNow for case 1: order t0,t1,t2,...,t9. required={t1,t4}. Process: t0 (remove), attempts1. Then list: t1,t2,t3,t4,t5,t6,t7,t8,t9. i=0. Next: t1 (required) -> i=1, attempts2. t2 (remove) -> i=1, attempts3. list: t1,t3,t4,t5,t6,t7,t8,t9. i=1 -> t3 (remove) -> i=1, attempts4. list: t1,t4,t5,t6,t7,t8,t9. i=1 -> t4 (required) -> i=2, attempts5. i=2 -> t5 (remove) -> i=2, attempts6. list: t1,t4,t6,t7,t8,t9. i=2 -> t6 (remove) -> attempts7, i=2. list: t1,t4,t7,t8,t9. i=2 -> t7 (remove) -> attempts8, i=2. list: t1,t4,t8,t9. i=2 -> t8 (remove) -> attempts9, i=2. list: t1,t4,t9. i=2 -> t9 (remove) -> attempts10, i=2. list: t1,t4. i=2 >= len=2, end. So attempts=10. Same.\n\nCase 2 similar.\n\nThus all result in attempts=10, one_minimal=False, reduced_items = required_failure_items in original order.\n\nSo the implementation can be as above.\n\nBut is there any other interpretation? Could the attempts count the number of \"removals\" (successful) only? That would be 8 for case 0, 8 for case 1, etc. But then one_minimal false still. However, the schema says attempts is integer, not necessarily number of removals. The term \"attempts\" suggests all tries, including failures.\n\nGiven the lack of counterexamples, I'll go with this.\n\nNow we need to write the Python function solve(case) that returns the object. We must include it in a JSON with key \"implementation\". The code must be a valid string in JSON.\n\nWrite